{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/bash -lc 'cat /Users/johnlee/.local/state/stamped-ai-research-next/preparation-20261015/cold-stamped-op-coordinator/studies/narps/coordinator-guidance/judging.md\ncat /Users/johnlee/.local/state/stamped-ai-research-next/preparation-20261015/cold-stamped-op-coordinator/studies/narps/coordinator-guidance/participant-preparation/upstream-methods.json\ncat /Users/johnlee/.local/state/stamped-ai-research-next/preparation-20261015/cold-stamped-op-coordinator/studies/narps/coordinator-guidance/participant-preparation/verification.json\ncat /Users/johnlee/.local/state/stamped-ai-research-next/preparation-20261015/cold-stamped-op-coordinator/studies/narps/coordinator-guidance/participant-preparation/independent-retrieval.json\ncat /Users/johnlee/.local/state/stamped-ai-research-next/preparation-20261015/cold-stamped-op-coordinator/studies/narps/coordinator-guidance/participant-preparation/annex-publication.json'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/bash -lc 'cat /Users/johnlee/.local/state/stamped-ai-research-next/preparation-20261015/cold-stamped-op-coordinator/studies/narps/coordinator-guidance/judging.md\ncat /Users/johnlee/.local/state/stamped-ai-research-next/preparation-20261015/cold-stamped-op-coordinator/studies/narps/coordinator-guidance/participant-preparation/upstream-methods.json\ncat /Users/johnlee/.local/state/stamped-ai-research-next/preparation-20261015/cold-stamped-op-coordinator/studies/narps/coordinator-guidance/participant-preparation/verification.json\ncat /Users/johnlee/.local/state/stamped-ai-research-next/preparation-20261015/cold-stamped-op-coordinator/studies/narps/coordinator-guidance/participant-preparation/independent-retrieval.json\ncat /Users/johnlee/.local/state/stamped-ai-research-next/preparation-20261015/cold-stamped-op-coordinator/studies/narps/coordinator-guidance/participant-preparation/annex-publication.json'","aggregated_output":"# Scientific assessment context\n\nAssess the original NARPS Hypothesis 1: a positive parametric effect of potential gain in vmPFC for the equal-indifference group.\nThe original [analysis instructions](https://www.narps.info/files/narps_instructions_for_analysis_teams.pdf) require whole-brain-corrected inference.\nThe scientific task is in `input/study-task.md`.\n\nWorkers receive 54 participant gain-effect maps from team AO86 and the corresponding cohort metadata.\nPreprocessing and participant modeling are fixed; evaluate group inference conditional on those inputs.\nJudge whether the analysis handles the intended participants, image geometry, quality, gain scaling, uncertainty and anatomical interpretation appropriately.\nThe maps represent independent participants; runs have already been combined within each person.\nEvaluate exclusions, uncertainty and multiple-comparison correction against the actual method.\nDefensible approaches can reach different conclusions; matching a prior team's answer is not the criterion.\nKeep scientific validity separate from the frozen STAMPED assessment.\nCheck how image coverage and missing observations enter the analysis; whether spatial alignment, vmPFC definition, group-model assumptions and whole-brain correction support the claimed inference; and whether quality checks, uncertainty and figures make those decisions inspectable.\nDo not require a particular mask, atlas, model or numerical result.\nParticipant variance maps are absent; judge methods against the supplied data.\n\n## Input preparation and rehearsal\n\n`prepare_subject_inputs.py` selects all 54 original single-subject Hypothesis 1 effect maps from [NeuroVault collection 4932](https://neurovault.org/collections/4932/), totaling 96,387,264 bytes.\nTheir participant IDs exactly match the equal-indifference group in pinned OpenNeuro ds001734 version 1.0.4.\n`input/sources.json` records the source URLs, cohort and methods revisions, file sizes and SHA256 hashes.\n`input/upstream-methods.md` explains the fixed model and its scaling limits; participant variance maps are not supplied.\nThe task makes no claim to reproduce or evaluate choices in the upstream time-series analysis.\n\n`participant-preparation/` retains selected source metadata, verification and annex retrieval evidence.\n`rehearse.py` times an intercept-only group model, whole-brain correction and figure generation; `rehearsal/` and `rehearsal-replay/` retain its outputs and timings.\nThe repeated numerical arrays matched exactly.\nThis establishes computational feasibility on the recorded host, not benchmark suitability, an agent completion time, a prescribed method or a definitive anatomical answer to Hypothesis 1.\nThree preparation pilots completed in approximately five to six minutes.\nThe user accepted NARPS as the first formal study: it tests a compact group-inference task, without a minimum compute duration.\nAcceptance does not establish scientific validity or a 30–45 minute workload.\nKeep these preparation runs separate from the formal comparison.\nResearch agents must not receive these rehearsal outputs.\n\n## Retained reference material\n\n`team-results/` preserves the former agreement-task package, including its prompt and source manifest.\nThose team maps and decisions are prior answers to Hypothesis 1 and are excluded from worker inputs.\n`prepare_team_results.py` regenerates this reference package from `published/`.\nThe existing verification, retrieval and publication receipts describe the paths at commit `2658c4759dac2d3817a1895444459adadadcd239`; their original evidence is retained unchanged.\n{\n  \"teamID\": \"AO86\",\n  \"n_participants\": 108,\n  \"used_fmriprep_data\": \"Yes\",\n  \"data_submitted_to_model\": \"108 participants were submitted to statistical modelling. Each participant had four runs of the MGT task from one session. No participants were excluded from analysis.\",\n  \"independent_vars_first_level\": \"Trials were separated into \\\"response\\\" and \\\"no response\\\". Response trials were modeled with a duration equal to the trial's response time, while no response trials had durations equal to the duration of the stimulus (4 seconds). Response trials were also modeled with two parametric modulators, in addition to a \\\"main effect of response\\\" regressor. In the \\\"main effect of response\\\" regressor, onsets and durations of response trials were convolved with a canonical SPM HRF, with no amplitude modulation. In the first parametric modulator regressor, response*response, participant responses (\\\"strongly reject\\\", \\\"weakly reject\\\", \\\"weakly accept\\\", and \\\"strongly accept\\\") were coded as numerical values (0, 1, 2, and 3, respectively). In order to orthogonalize the response*response regressor, the participant response values were also mean-centered and variance-normalized, and were included as amplitude modulators, prior to convolution with a canonical SPM HRF. The second parametric modulator depended on the model.\\n\\nTwo models were fitted for each participant: one for gain and one for loss. For the gain model, gain values for response trials were used to modulate amplitude of the response*gain parametrically modulator regressor, after mean-centering and variance-normalization. For the loss trials, the same procedure was performed for loss values and a response*loss regressor was included in the model. This resulted in three to four task regressors for each model: \\\"main effect of response\\\", \\\"response*response\\\", \\\"response*gain\\\" or \\\"response*loss\\\", and sometimes \\\"no response\\\".\\n\\nA cosine drift model was used for high-pass filtering with a cutoff of 128 seconds, which resulted in six drift regressors included in each model. Other nuisance regressors included in the model were: six translational and rotational motion parameters, their temporal derivatives, and censored volumes indicating high motion, proximity to high motion, or non-steady state. An AR(1) autocorrelation model was also included in the models. No orthogonalization was applied to any task or nuisance regressors.\\n\\nRun-level models were fitted for each run and then combined across runs, per participant, with a fixed-effects model (i.e.,an average) combining parameter estimates for the response*gain and response*loss regressors across runs.   \",\n  \"analysis_SW_with_version\": \"FSL 5.0.7, nistats 0.0.1b\"\n}\n{\n  \"verified_at\": \"2026-10-15T20:07:22.475038+00:00\",\n  \"elapsed_seconds\": 14.907914124894887,\n  \"versions\": {\n    \"python\": \"3.13.16\",\n    \"nibabel\": \"5.4.2\",\n    \"numpy\": \"2.5.4\",\n    \"pandas\": \"3.0.6\"\n  },\n  \"input_manifest_sha256\": \"919be9b4278c31f7e39ae3d02fcf159eb4ae222a2fb96c90df7c3a92e5fb19cd\",\n  \"all_hashes_match\": true,\n  \"cohort_matches\": true,\n  \"all_maps_finite\": true,\n  \"common_geometry\": true,\n  \"affine\": [\n    [\n      2.0,\