{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_29","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '80,260p' /Users/johnlee/.local/state/stamped-ai-research-next/stamped-judge-7enxbwl9/skills/stamped-conventions/references/response-format.md\ncat /Users/johnlee/.local/state/stamped-ai-research-next/stamped-judge-7enxbwl9/skills/stamped-conventions/references/project-kinds.md\n\"","aggregated_output":"\n> Produced by `assess-alignment-with-stamped-principles` 0.1.0 with `stamped-conventions` 0.1.0, checklist 0.3.1 (`stamped-checklist-schema@v0.3.1`, `af259ba`), on <object url> at commit `<sha>`, 2026-10-13, <harness>/<model>.\n> Probe output: `<path to the probe JSON>`.\n\nA report without the stamp cannot be compared against another iteration, so it is not finished.\n\n## Report layout\n\nUse this order, so the reader meets the actionable part first:\n\n1. **Verdict line** — `MUST: n met / n partial / n unmet / n not-checked`, then SHOULD and MAY, each with its denominator.\n   No single overall score, ever.\n2. **What to fix first** — at most five, highest leverage first, each as *what to change* → *why it matters to this project* → *the item ids it closes*.\n3. **What the project already does well** — two or three, with citations.\n4. **Per-item table** — MUST first, then SHOULD, then MAY: id, principle codes, status, evidence, reason.\n   `scripts/encode_response.py table <response>.json` renders exactly this from the response; it is not a table to type by hand.\n5. **Flagged items** — every item carrying a `flags` entry, with one line each: what the probe measured versus what the item asks, or what the override was.\n   An empty list is worth stating as \"none\".\n6. **Not checked** — each with what would settle it.\n7. **Goals and kind** — the project's stated goals (and where they came from: documentation, or the user's answers), its kind, and the emphasis that follows.\n8. **The checklist URL**, the stamp, and a pointer to the response JSON.\n   The URL is ~8–10 kB of base64 for a full 31-item response, so the report **links** `<date>-url.txt` rather than inlining it; a link a reader can click, not a wall they have to scroll.\n\n## The checklist-app URL\n\nFacts, from [stamped-checklist](https://github.com/stamped-principles/stamped-checklist) at `d24d7ca`:\n\n- `PERSISTENCE_FORMAT = 3` (`src/persistence.js`).\n- The `responses` parameter is **standard base64** (not URL-safe) of UTF-8 JSON `{\"checklist_version\", \"responses\"}`, then percent-encoded as a query parameter.\n- The app builds `?checklist=<v>&responses_version=<v>&cols=auto&sections=off&format=3&responses=<b64>` (`src/script.js`, `syncPersistentURL`).\n- It accepts `value ∈ {\"yes\",\"no\",null}`, requires `reason` to be a **string** (empty is fine), truncates it to 250 characters, shows it only for `\"no\"`, drops keys it does not know, and **rejects the whole load** on an id that is not in the requested checklist version.\n\n`scripts/encode_response.py` is the only writer of that URL, and `decode` is the reverse — that is how a checklist a person filled in the web app becomes reference answers for calibration, with no extra labelling tool.\n\n## When the full assessment record matters\n\nThe response JSON is the canonical assessment record. When a calling evaluation harness supplies pinned export schemas, derive its additional records from that JSON; do not invent formats or make an absent exporter block the assessment.\n# Project kinds and emphasis\n\nSettle the kind **before** judging the object, from its documentation and the user's answers, not from a guess.\nAsk when it is not plain; a wrong kind moves the emphasis and produces a confidently wrong report.\nThe kind is recorded in the response JSON (`object.kind`) and, for eval runs, as the `rubric`.\n\n| Kind | What it is | Emphasis | Typically `not-applicable` |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **dataset** | Data as the product: raw, derivative, or both | **M**odularity (raw vs derived vs code), **P**ortability of whatever produced it, **D**istributability (retrievable by others, licence stated) | E.1 only if the data was not computed at all (acquired raw data) |\n| **component / tool** | Code others call: a library, a container recipe, a workflow | **E**phemerality (each execution in a disposable environment), **P**ortability, **A**ctionability (tests and an entry point) | Nothing by default; a tool with no results of its own answers must/006 about its own test runs |\n| **study** | A question answered with data and compute: the usual shape of these skills' output | All seven; **T** (every derived file has a re-executable record) and **S** (inputs under the boundary as subdatasets, not paths) carry the most weight | Rarely anything |\n| **skill / agent harness** | A skill, prompt collection or agent configuration — including the skills in this collection | **T** (`version:`, changelog), **A** (tests outside the skill directory), **P** (no host assumptions), **D** (installable at a pin) | may/001 where there are no external modules |\n\n## A shape that comes up constantly\n\n**A study whose product is a re-derivation from another dataset's derivatives** —\nit consumes published results, adds a join, a table and a figure, and produces no\nraw or intermediate data of its own. It is a **study**, not a dataset: the product\nis an answer, and the inputs belong to someone else.\nWhat follows: S.1 and M.1 are judged on whether those inputs are *linked* rather than copied;\nT is judged on the few derived files it does make, and \"few\" is not a discount;\nE.1 is weighed lightly if the computation is seconds of CPU, and never counted against the object for being cheap.\n\n## Mixed objects\n\nMost real projects are more than one kind.\nName the **primary** kind, list the secondary ones, and judge each item against the component it applies to — a study that ships a reusable converter is judged on E.1 for the converter and on T for the results.\nSay in the report which component each verdict was read from.\n\n## What the emphasis changes, and what it does not\n\n- It changes **what to fix first** (report order), and the \"why it matters\" sentence attached to each fix.\n- It changes which items are honestly `not-applicable`.\n- It does **not** change a MUST into a SHOULD, and it never lowers the evidence bar.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
